{"id": "sllama0188", "pool": "sllama", "arm": "local", "tier": "never", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 2121542183, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "53872f8980a0e30128a35991b38d3f263a90256c840f85d9399ea7b7c71d66dc", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": null, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 0, "query": "Who is the supreme god in Hinduism?", "input_path": "/data/bench_audio/sllama0188.wav", "input_sha256": "e208704941da39600e137df1aa4361046c9b4781f45f77111f3ed65e119eb4df", "input_samples": 43096, "input_s": 2.693, "n_real_chunks": 3, "score": 0.2648, "act": 0.9997, "is_info": true, "fired": false, "mode": "local", "answer": "In Hinduism, there isn' t a single supreme god like in some other religions. Instead, Hindu ism is poly theistic, meaning it worships many gods and god desses. However, there are three main aspects of the divine that are often considered central: Bra hman, Vishnu, and S iv a.Brah man represents the ultimate reality or the un i versal spirit, but it's not always personified as a specific god. Vish nu is seen as the pres erver and maintainer of the universe, often depicted with four arms and associated with balance and order. Shiva is the destroyer and transformer, responsible for renewal through destruction. People often worship these deities depending on their needs or beliefs. So while there's no one \"supreme\" god in the traditional sense, Vi shnu and Shi va are commonly regarded as major figures who embody different aspects of the divine.", "relay_text": "", "expert_answer": "", "transcript": "", "asr_s": null, "expert_s": null, "expert_input_samples": null, "expert_input_s": null, "expert_input_sha256": null, "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": null, "relay_synth_ms": null, "onset_chunk": 3, "eot_seen": true, "n_speak_chunks": 42, "feed_lag_max_ms": 0, "feed_lag_sum_ms": 0, "audio_files": {"local": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real